Andrew Kerr <andrewkerruk@spamguard.yahoo.co.uk> writes:
> The closed source clients and server know a passphrase. On connection, 
> the server sends a challenge including random salt. The client combines 
> the salt and passphrase, feeds the result through a hash function, and 
> sends the hash to the server.

I think I'd prefer we say "precompiled" rather than "closed source".
The client may be FOSS, but for the tournament players would be required
to use one of the "official" builds with the right key compiled in.

If the precompiled clients know a passphrase, then the passphrase in
question is public. There is nothing short of physical control over the
hardware on which the client runs which can change this.

IMHO, all you're doing is giving additional incentive to prospective
cheaters by presenting a challenge and bragging rights.

> It doesn't need to be unbreakable. It needs to be as hard to cheat as it 
> is now, since we already know that the current level of protection is 
> sufficient.

Precisely right. I just don't see a big distinction between precompiled
clients using some obfuscated protocol and telling people "play nice
and act like adults."


